    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Yamaha Previews the Future of the Tenere Adventure Bikes With Mysterious 4 Concept\nFuture Flight Global Agrees to Buy Up to 20 Alia All-Electric Aircraft from Beta\n7 photos\nPhoto: Future Flight Global/Instagram\nThe latest deal signed by Beta Technologies involves a reputable operator in the mobility management sector, and even the signing of the agreement was original and symbolic, marking the beginning of a new era where all-electric aircraft take center stage.\nSigning an in-flight agreement for the purchase of several all-electric aircraft is symbolic in itself, and there was no better way to do it for two CEOs that are also both pilots. Karan Singh, founder and CEO of Future Flight Global, is a Boeing 737 airline captain with 5,000 hours under his belt, while Kyle Clark, founder and CEO of Beta Technologies, is a Beta test pilot and certified flight instructor, with more than 200 test flights in the Alia aircraft alone.\nBeyond the strong symbolism of this signing up in the clouds, the agreement marks a serious commitment from FFG to purchase ten Alia aircraft, with the option for ten more units. The Alia CTOL, which is the conventional takeoff and landing version of the Alia all-electric aircraft, was at the center of this new purchase agreement.\nAlia CTOL’s biggest selling point is that, unlike its eVTOL (vertical takeoff and landing) counterpart, it’s compatible with existing airport infrastructure and doesn’t require dedicated infrastructure. This makes CTOLs much easier and faster to deploy in regular commercial operations.\nFor now, we know that FFG intends to operate these all-electric aircraft for regional mobility service, but the cooperation with Beta is meant to expand further than that. FFG hinted at potential joint operations in international markets at some point, and it’s also working on a AAM (Advanced Air Mobility) multipurpose model for a wide range of applications, from passenger transportation and eco-tourism to medical missions, defense logistics, and cargo.\nBeta’s Alia CTOL has already earned a solid reputation beyond US borders. This past summer, the UK-based Loganair has confirmed that it will become the first on the continent to operate a fleet of five all-electric aircraft, following a purchase agreement with Beta for five Alia aircraft, and the option of five more units.\nPrior to that, in 2023, Alia was the first of its kind to be officially selected for Air New Zealand’s Mission Next Gen Aircraft Program, a large-scale initiative designed to find the best clean flight alternatives that could eventually replace Air New Zealand’s conventional fleet. Air New Zealand officially ordered one Alia CTOL, with the option for two more, and has recently conducted tests as part of an intensive Demonstrator program.\nBack home, in the US, Beta has recently wrapped up its participation in the historic eIPP (eVTOL and electric aircraft Integration Pilot Program), and it was a blast. In Florida, the last state for Beta’s flight demonstrations, the Alia CTOL has completed 14 flights in the Orlando and Tampa regions, a total of almost 700 nautical miles.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAlia CTOL beta technologies Alia electric aircraft electric aircraft evtol eCTOL\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Otilia Drăgan\nOtilia believes that if it’s eco, green, or groundbreaking, people should know about it (especially if it's got wheels or wings). Working in online media for over five years, she's gained a deeper perspective on how people everywhere can inspire each other. Full profile\nUP NEXT Father-Son Project Must Go, 1957 Chevy Bel Air Already in Primer, Still a Big Restoration",
